e-investigation-forensics walkthrough log — 2026-10-10
Worked-case lesson: no command line by design (brief §6).
The "run" is the forensic-readiness assessment + investigation plan + chain-of-custody
procedure authored and self-reviewed in-session against NIST SP 800-86 (four phases:
collection, examination, analysis, reporting), RFC 3227 (order of volatility,
transparency, chain of custody §4.1), and the NIJ Electronic Crime Scene
Investigation guide for first responders (2nd ed.).

Scope: fictional Meridian Analytics (180 staff, continued from e-iam). Scenario:
departing sales engineer exfiltrates customer data to personal cloud storage over
two weeks; DLP alert fires on last-week Friday.
Artifacts authored (all fictional, labeled):
  - Readiness assessment: 4 components scored (90-day file-access log retention,
    forensic retainer 4h SLA, first-responder card, counsel-reviewed playbook);
    gap recorded honestly (personal cloud drive outside corporate visibility).
  - First-responder card content: order of volatility, "do not" list, call tree.
  - Chain-of-custody form design + excerpt (item MA-2026-041, SHA-256 at
    acquisition, sealed original, analysis on copies).
  - Investigation plan: counsel direction, litigation hold, collection in
    volatility order, premature-interview deferred in writing, analysis
    leap-check, three-track decision log (employment / criminal-referral /
    regulatory), two reports (technical + executive).

